I tend to agree.  One might think, restore ability should revert it to 
original form while gain ability lets it keep the new one.  But gain 
ability is already useful, and I think they wanted to make restore 
ability more useful.  In vanilla, its major use is as a source of water, 
once you have a unicorn horn...

OTOH, if I were an advocate, I could make a case for restore ability to 
work as it does.  When you polymorph an object in slashem, it acquires 
the property of "haziness", i.e., it does not have the full capabilities 
of the new object, it gets them only partly, and eventually, it has to 
revert to its original form.  Now, restore ability can only restore an 
ability that is presently there, not one that is absent.  A T shirt, for 
example, cannot rust.  The ability to rust cannot be recreated by 
restore ability, so it cannot change the T shirt back to the iron helm 
it used to be.  But it can restore the present abilities of the T shirt 
to their maximal capacity, i.e., it can remove haziness.  Afterwards, 
the T shirt is a normal T shirt without any tendency to revert to an 
iron helm.  According to this logic, gain ability should make it revert, 
as long as it is hazy ... (which it does not, as far as I know!).
